WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.240
2005's Fantastic Four may not be a film&nbsp;
Marvel fans-or superhero fans in general-want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.360
to remember, but it exists&nbsp;
whether they like it or not.&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:15.280
2007's Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer&nbsp;
didn't do much to improve upon its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.360
and 2015's reboot Fantastic Four unfortunately&nbsp;
followed that same downward trend. With another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.320
Fantastic Four reboot on the way, fans can only&nbsp;
hope that another film starring Marvel's First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.720
Family doesn't go the way of the Negative Zone.
Part of what could make that happen is having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.280
the actors and director work well together,&nbsp;
which was not the case for Rise of the Silver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.040
Surfer. Indeed, Jessica Alba-who played Susan&nbsp;
Storm, aka the Invisible Woman-didn't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.880
get along with director Tim Story. Alba had such&nbsp;
an unsavory time filming Rise of the Silver Surfer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.440
that one particularly difficult scene nearly&nbsp;
made her say goodbye to acting for good.&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.560
For a bit of context, Alba hasn't always been on&nbsp;
the best terms with screenwriters, being quoted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.080
Elle magazine's December 2010 issue as saying,
"Good actors never use the script unless it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.640
amazing writing. All the good actors I've worked&nbsp;
with, they all say whatever they want to say."&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.360
This caught the attention of many writers in&nbsp;
Hollywood-most notably Charlie's Angels scribe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.400
John August, who wrote a blog post in an attempt&nbsp;
to explain why Alba's comments were hurtful and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.120
quote, "setting dangerous expectations."
According to Alba, that quote was taken out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.800
context, as she explained during a press event for&nbsp;
2010's Little Fockers. It's not that Alba thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.280
scripts don't matter-it's that she personally&nbsp;
feels she doesn't know how to not be tied to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.320
script and to improvise when needed, which she&nbsp;
apparently considers the mark of a good actor.&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.680
Alba went on to say,
"Basically, I was saying that I didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.240
the courage and didn't really understand how to&nbsp;
bring my own thing to the table and I would never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.840
veer away from the script, ever, no matter what."
This confession from Alba ties into her experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.720
on Rise of the Silver Surfer. She reportedly had&nbsp;
a number of "experiences where she was told to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.560
read the words on the page" in the exact&nbsp;
way they were written; that made her lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.440
confidence in herself and her acting abilities.
"It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.160
nice to be wanted sometimes, Reed."
During one of the emotional climaxes in Rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.120
of the Silver Surfer, Alba's character, Sue, is&nbsp;
crying as she dies in her husband Reed Richards'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.000
arms. But the sequence seen on the big screen&nbsp;
and the way it was originally shot are completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.320
different. Alba acted out the crying scene,&nbsp;
perhaps the way it was written in the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.120
but it wasn't what the director wanted.&nbsp;
According to Alba, Story told her to quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.280
"be prettier" during the scene.
Alba explained to Elle magazine,&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.080
"I remember when I was dying in Silver Surfer...&nbsp;
The director was like, 'It looks too real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.720
It looks too painful. Can you be prettier&nbsp;
when you cry? Cry pretty, Jessica, [...] Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.680
make it flat. We can CGI the tears in.'"
A director critiquing an actor’s work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.640
asking for some degree of adjustment is one thing,&nbsp;
but to take advantage of her looks rather than her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.400
emotional range and say animators will digitally&nbsp;
modify her performance? It's hard to imagine what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.760
that felt like. At the time, the experience made&nbsp;
Alba question not only her worth as an actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.280
but also her worth as a human being.
As she told Elle,&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.240
"It all got me thinking, Am I not good enough?&nbsp;
Are my instincts and my emotions not good enough?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.440
Do people hate them so much that&nbsp;
they don't want me to be a person?"&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.680
From there, Alba began to feel differently&nbsp;
about the acting profession as a whole:&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.200
"I just said, 'F--- it. I don't&nbsp;
care about this business anymore.'"&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.840
Thankfully, she didn't end up quitting acting,&nbsp;
explaining to Elle that motherhood gave her a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.840
thicker skin and helped her become more&nbsp;
confident in the face of criticism.&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.120
Since the release of Rise of the Silver&nbsp;
Surfer in June 2007, Alba married Cash Warren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.960
whom she met in 2004 while filming Fantastic Four,&nbsp;
became a mother of three, and has been in numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.240
films and TV shows. Some of her most notable&nbsp;
roles include Cam Wexler in Good Luck Chuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.960
Nancy Callahan in Sin City: A Dame to Kill For,&nbsp;
and Victoria Knox in Barely Lethal. And from 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:57.440
to 2020, Alba held a main role as Nancy McKenna&nbsp;
in the Bad Boys spin-off series L.A.'s Finest.&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.560
Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer may have&nbsp;
pushed Alba to nearly quit acting, but we're glad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.280
she didn't let the tough time dull her shine.
